{
  "item": "Grass03",
  "mu_name": "Grass",
  "mu_name_from": "Lorencia's object slot 22. WorldObjectCatalog.cs runs Grass as a block of eight from 20, so Grass03.bmd is type 22.",
  "world": "lorencia",
  "object_type": 22,
  "why_this_one": "Placement count. two sheets: a broad leaf and a finer one. Lorencia stands 1031 clumps of grass between the seven kinds - 36% of everything in the town - and each one is a handful of crossed cards wearing a cut-out sheet, so the cheapest assets in the map are also the ones that fill most of it.",
  "profile": "world",
  "sheets": {
    "tree_01": "tree_01.tga.png",
    "tree_02": "tree_02.tga.png"
  },
  "sheet_from": "Data/Object1/, from MuMain. MU has three stems that exist twice over, as an .OZJ and an .OZT that are not the same picture: tree_01 is furrowed bark as a JPEG and a leaf clump as a TGA, tree_02 is birch bark or moss, tree_04 is a sawn log end or pink blossom. The .bmd says which by extension and both halves are genuinely in use across Lorencia, so the decoded files carry the extension in their names and neither gets the bare one.",
  "sheet_materials": {
    "tree_01": "foliage",
    "tree_02": "foliage"
  },
  "material_note": "foliage throughout, which is what it is. No normal map: the geometry is a flat card and the shape the eye reads is the alpha, so embossing a leaf texture across it announces that it is a picture printed on a board.",
  "cutout": [
    "tree_01",
    "tree_02"
  ],
  "cutout_from": "Every sheet carries a real alpha channel, cutting away between 56% and 77% of its square. Declared rather than sniffed, for the reason in tests/cutouts.py.",
